            in output of one row with multiple columns instead of a               single column spread sheet.          -s cell               Start the top left corner of the spread sheet in _c_e_l_l.               For example, -_s _B_3_3 will arrange the output data so               that the spread sheet starts in column B, row 33.          -R n Increment by _n on each new output row.                                   - 1 -   Formatted:  August 15, 1991     PPPPSSSSCCCC((((1111))))                                                     PPPPSSSSCCCC((((1111))))          -C n Increment by _n on each new output column.          -n n Output _n rows before advancing to the next column.               This option is used when the input is arranged in a               single column and the spread sheet is to have multiple               columns, each of which is to be length _n.          -d c Use the single character _c as the delimiter between               input fields.          -P   Plain numbers only.  A field is a number only when               there is no imbeded [-+eE].          -S   All numbers are strings.          -v   Print the version of psc     SSSSEEEEEEEE AAAALLLLSSSSOOOO          sc(1)     AAAAUUUUTTTTHHHHOOOORRRR          Robert Bond                                   - 2 -   Formatted:  August 15, 1991bmerh639-7> 